  <p>
    <strong>Sarcoidosis</strong> is not a disease of the adrenal, and it is not caused by a
    melanocortin mutation. It is here because one of the oldest melanocortin drugs still
    carries it on the label. Repository corticotropin — ACTH in a slow-release gel, sold as
    Acthar — is approved in the United States for symptomatic sarcoidosis, alongside a list
    of other inflammatory indications that long predates the cloning of the
    receptors.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>What the disease is</h2>
  <p>
    In sarcoidosis the immune system builds granulomas: tight clusters of macrophages and
    other cells. They form most often in the lungs and the lymph nodes in the chest, and
    they can form in skin, eye, heart and nerve. Some people clear the granulomas and never
    need a drug. Others develop scarring, breathlessness, or organ damage that does. When
    treatment is required, the drug clinicians reach for first is usually a glucocorticoid.
    The trouble with that, over years, is the trouble with excess steroid.
  </p>

  <h2>Why an ACTH gel is on the label</h2>
  <p>
    ACTH is cut from <a href="/pomc">POMC</a>. Given as a drug it is a melanocortin agonist,
    and its classical job is to bind <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a> on the adrenal cortex
    and release cortisol. On that reading, Acthar is an indirect steroid: you inject the
    signal, the adrenal makes the hormone.<Cite n={4} /> The product has been used since the
    1950s, which is why the label looks nothing like the labels of{' '}
    <a href="/setmelanotide">setmelanotide</a> or <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interesting claim is that this is not the whole story. Melanocortin receptors also
    sit on immune cells, and a steroid-independent anti-inflammatory action through those
    receptors has been proposed for conditions where steroids alone disappoint. How much of
    the clinical effect is truly independent of cortisol is still argued, and the product
    has also drawn scrutiny for its cost.<Cite n={4} /> The mechanism, and the rest of the
    Acthar story, is on the <a href="/therapeutics#corticotropin">therapeutics page</a>. The
    sister labelled use in acute <a href="/multiple-sclerosis-relapse">multiple-sclerosis
    relapses</a> raises the same question.
  </p>

  <h2>What the newer studies can and cannot say</h2>
  <p>
    A 2017 single-blind study enrolled people with chronic pulmonary sarcoidosis whose lung
    function had slipped while they were on prednisone. They received a short loading course
    of the gel and were then randomised to 40 or 80 units twice a week. Sixteen people
    finished 24 weeks. Prednisone doses fell, diffusing capacity and a quality-of-life score
    improved, and the PET signal in lung lesions dropped. Forced vital capacity did not
    change significantly, and the two doses did not separate.<Cite n={2} /> That is a
    steroid-sparing signal in a very small, unblinded-to-treatment study.
  </p>
  <p>
    A later multicentre trial (NCT03320070) did use a placebo. Fifty-five people with
    pulmonary sarcoidosis were randomised to 80 units twice weekly or placebo for 24 weeks,
    on top of usual care, with a planned taper of glucocorticoids. The study was stopped
    early because enrollment collapsed during the COVID-19 pandemic, so it was not powered
    for a statistical verdict. The trends — a sarcoidosis treatment score, and more people
    coming off glucocorticoids — favoured the gel, and no new safety signal was
    reported.<Cite n={3} /> An underpowered trend is not a proof. It is where the evidence
    actually stands.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Sarcoidosis belongs on this site as a labelled use of an ACTH drug, not as a melanocortin
    disease in the sense that <a href="/congenital-adrenal-hyperplasia">CAH</a> or{' '}
    <a href="/epp">erythropoietic protoporphyria</a> are. The biology that makes the label
    plausible is real: ACTH is a POMC peptide, MC2R releases cortisol, and other melanocortin
    receptors sit on immune cells. The clinical case for reaching for the gel instead of a
    steroid is much thinner than the case for the newer, receptor-selective drugs elsewhere
    on the <a href="/conditions">conditions map</a>.
  </p>

